=== HINT 1: Fill the guarded bodies ===
Inside each guarded block put the risky operation:
  result = a / b;      (safe_divide)
  val = *ptr;          (safe_deref, MSVC/Clang path)
In the handler body / after catching, produce the fallback:
  safe_divide: return INT_MIN;   safe_deref: fall through, val stays 0.

=== HINT 2: How the MinGW path works ===
GCC cannot compile MS SEH syntax. excpt.h provides __try1(filter)/__except1:
the filter gets EXCEPTION_POINTERS* and returns EXCEPTION_EXECUTE_HANDLER (1)
to catch or EXCEPTION_CONTINUE_SEARCH (0) to pass the exception up. The
provided divzero_filter already does this for divide-by-zero - just wire the
bodies. MinGW allows only ONE such scope per .c file (fixed asm labels),
which is why only safe_divide is SEH-guarded here.

=== HINT 3 (SPOILER): safe_deref on MinGW ===
Probe the page before touching it (VirtualQuery, see wi03):
  MEMORY_BASIC_INFORMATION mbi;
  if (ptr == NULL || VirtualQuery(ptr, &mbi, sizeof(mbi)) != sizeof(mbi) ||
      mbi.State != MEM_COMMIT)
      return 0;
  val = *ptr;
MSVC/Clang users can instead wrap val = *ptr in __try/__except with a filter
on EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION, and can additionally demo __finally.
